<p>Alright, looks good, but I do have questions. Warning: long and possibly nitpicky.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Collapsed&nbsp;for&nbsp;length</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-4000-América<br />
Mobile Task Force Xi-Kai<br />
SCP-4000-Barcelona<br />
EoI-4000-Cáceres</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Is there any reason for these designations, instead of, say, -1, -2, and an MTF number? This isn't a criticism, mind you, I just thought these were unusual choices, and I can't see the logic behind them.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Fresco #0010 to be an anachronistic depiction of a modern atom under the Bohr model, although the nature of the diagram prevents conclusive identification of what element is being depicted.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In addition to missing a word, surely the number of rings in the diagram would narrow the range of possibilities greatly?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>However, they did note traces of plutonium-239 and uranium-235 isotopes in the soil.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Those would probably be found at a bomb construction site, rather than at a detonation. Nuclear fallout is made up of a whole mess of different radioactive materials.</p>
<blockquote>
