<blockquote>
<p>Those would probably be found at a bomb construction site, rather than at a detonation. Nuclear fallout is made up of a whole mess of different radioactive materials.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm the one that suggested that line be changed to what it is now, my reasoning being that the radioisotopes U-235 and Pu-239, used in most atomic bombs (atomic, not hydrogen) stay around for quite a while even after detonation, given that U-235 has a half-life of 703.8 million years and Pu-239 has a half-life of 24,100 years. These isotopes are present both at construction sites (but not to a strong degree, or else someone's violating the local health physicist's wishes) and after detonation.</p>
<p>That said, for U-235, it would be reasonable to also throw in some detections of <a href="https://qph.fs.quoracdn.net/main-qimg-ad2ea8c669e7138e5ee166947866c71e">other long-lived isotopes from its decay chain</a>, given how much time has passed and the fact that a lot of fissioning has already happened (and in accordance with your accurate statement that fallout is &quot;a whole mess of different radioactive materials&quot;).</p>

