<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Response</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Is there any reason for these designations, instead of, say, -1, -2, and an MTF number? This isn't a criticism, mind you, I just thought these were unusual choices, and I can't see the logic behind them.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Concerning the subdesignations: The primary reason (which applies both in universe and out) is that codenames are easier to remember and distinguish than numbers.</p>
<p>In the earliest stages of this draft, the subdesignations were Greek letters, which is something that both I and other authors have done before. However, since this SCP is set in Mexico, and all the characters are either Hispanic or Mayan,<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-286776-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-286776-1')">1</a></sup> I didn't like the Greek characters and wanted to use something different. I settled on a Spanish phonetic dictionary, which I felt was appropriate.</p>
<p>Yes, it's an unusual choice, but it's something I chose to give it a little more spice, and also based on a general desire to have things be more immediately recognizable.</p>
<p>As for Xi-Kai, that one wasn't my decision: that MTF was created by <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/maliceaforethought" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2902864); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2902864&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1531872619" alt="MaliceAforethought" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2902864)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/maliceaforethought" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2902864); return false;">MaliceAforethought</a></span> and used in <a href="/scp-2668">SCP-2668</a> and <a href="/scp-3716">SCP-3716</a>. I can't say why he chose that designation. I considered crosslinking to one of those, but ended up choosing not to - I still might.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In addition to missing a word, surely the number of rings in the diagram would narrow the range of possibilities greatly?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I've since clarified that section to really show what I mean by it &#8212; basically, while it is a representation of the Bohr model, it's not a very <em>good</em> representation of the Bohr model. The number of valence rings clearly doesn't match up with the depicted size of the nucleus, the electrons are missing, etc.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Those would probably be found at a bomb construction site, rather than at a detonation. Nuclear fallout is made up of a whole mess of different radioactive materials.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not the most knowledgeable about the exact composition of nuclear fallout, but those two isotopes were chosen in part because their half-lives were long enough that there'd still be some left 1,300 years after an explosion, and because they are used in the construction of a nuclear bomb. (Also because Cyantreuse recommended them.) If you have other isotopes that could possibly be found, please suggest them!</p>
<p><strong>EDIT:</strong> Cyan posted while I was writing this response, I've added a mention that other isotopes formed from the decay chain of U235 were also found.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A good response. Beyond the anomalous use of a subcritical mass, there is little danger here. See the &quot;Summary Report of the NTH Country Experiment&quot; for more info.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Pretty much. 4000-América is a rather small collection of nuclear bombs, and none of them are even that big.</p>
